A||||23||V8Fiero||Poll: Worst fiero repair job? I know mine!!||||5||
Z||000000||V8Fiero||05-20-2001||01:16 PM||||Just got done replacing an oil pan gasket.....<P>Holy Crap!!!<P>Never in a million yrs wouldve I thought it would be that involved. Worked on it for 4 hours Friday night. 5 Hours Sat. afternoon and finally finished it up ( along with one side of brake pad ) at 2 am Sunday morning.<P>What are some of your worst repair jobs?<P>Scott||||reg||5
Z||000001||sjp777||05-20-2001||01:30 PM||||Front side V6 exhaust manifold. Had a huge crack. Broke 2 bolts off in the head taking it off. Managed to get 1 out without too much problem. Spent 2 days trying to drill a hole deep enough for an ez out in the other. Finally got the hole, and promptly broke the ez out off inside. At this point I gave up. Now I have replaced the ticking sound from the crack in the manifold with the same ticking sound from the missing exhaust manifold bolt.<P>Yipee<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_2.jpg">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A><p>[This message has been edited by sjp777 (edited 05-20-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000002||Stoic||05-20-2001||01:34 PM||||Well, the first one that comes to mind is what I thought was going to be simple.  The deck lid hinge (driver side).  Mine snapped, I'm not sure why.  It wasn't rusted, just worn I guess.  But on top of the fact that the screw holding on the hinge is a special kind (can't remember the name), the local hardware store wouldn't sell me a single socket.  They wanted to sell me the whole set ($80!).  I said forget that.  But the real fun part was putting the new hinge on.  I threw many tools in frusteration, some of which I still have not found.  It's amazing how some of the most frusterating can be the most trivial things.  <P>------------------<BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||spideyfoot||05-20-2001||01:54 PM||||#3 spark plug last weekend. Damage done:<BR>busted knuckle (maandatory), lost dzus fastener right side vent, missing two wire loom halves.<BR>3 days two remove stuck threads, removal of hood, right vent, drilling chipping ezouting remaining plug chunk. <BR>Extra tools<BR>EZ Outs, Chisles, Punches, Cheap 1/2" DEEP SOCKET SET, PSBlaster, <BR>One shot Khackie short, Fox hat, ...||||reg||1
Z||000004||PBJ||05-20-2001||03:14 PM||||Just like sjp777, I did the frt manifold gasket on my 86. Went like a dream.  Go to do it to my wife's and drilling out a broken bolt and the drill bit breaks in the hole.  All in all though, I have found working on my fieros have been pretty good other than the stiff back from reaching over the rear trunk.  The exhaust tick is just starting to return. 1 month later.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>But we still love'm||||reg||1
Z||000005||Taijiguy||05-20-2001||04:28 PM||||Replacing the clutch with the motor still in. Sitting on the engine wrestling the trans back into position- (no lift or hoist.) Not fun.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fformula88||05-20-2001||04:45 PM||||My uncle would certainly say its replacing the clutch!  He did one in my dads 85 SE 2m6, and it took him from a Friday night thru Sunday afternoon of almost constant working... and this is WITH a lift!  hehe, not an easy job.  <P>My personal fav was the rear calipers on my 88.  First it took me forever to get them off.  Why GM used those stupid pins you have to pound out is beyond reason.  Then I had a hard time getting the pistons bottomed out!  They went, so I put it back together and they started leaking, so then I had to rip them back apart again!  I rebuilt the calipers, and whoever had put them together before me had the inner o-ring in backwards, and it broke bottoming the piston.  Finally rebuilt and ready to go, I was able to get it all back together and working.  Took about two weeks total from start to finish (inc. waiting for parts for a few of those days!).  But it was still better than the $800 I was quoted for the job from my mechanic!||||reg||1
Z||000007||KevinP||05-20-2001||05:12 PM||||my rears were a pain in the a$$ too, had to take both calipers to a machine shop to get the bolts out, them could never get the damn things to bottom out again!||||reg||4
Z||000008||Raydar||05-20-2001||05:46 PM||||My worst repair job, so far, would have to be the cross-car coolant pipe. That's the one under the front manifold on the V-6. <BR>Looking forward to doing the front manifold.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> Sounds like a Briggs and Stratton.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000009||TheFiero64||05-21-2001||11:00 AM||||Mine was the fuel pump  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I hope I never have to drop the tank again!<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fiero54||05-21-2001||11:09 AM||||Timing gear on an 84SE||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mr.Frosty||05-21-2001||12:32 PM||||I had to replace the right rear caliper after the piston wouldn't turn back in,then it decided to come out.Couldn't get it to go back in,and it got scored up real bad.So I ended up replacing the whole thing,which was easier than trying to rebuild it.The worst moment was when the piston came out(I was trying to turn it in and out....mistake!!!)A qaurt of brake fluid spilled out on my legs,tools,and hands.I had to have the car running before the next morning for work.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>I hate '88 rear calipers with a passion.<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top-Sold <BR>85 GT Turbo-project car||||reg||8
Z||000012||Greg Piet||05-21-2001||02:07 PM||||Redoing the rings with the engine still in the car...  in a carport.  I am so embarassed by that job for not doing it "right".<BR>Bad nasty dirty environment, didnt do any block work, just replaced the rings and slapped her back together.<P>At least I am making amends with my current motor rebuild!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Fiero5||05-21-2001||02:13 PM||||Timing gear seal!<BR>What a pain they are as the seal doesn't want to line up. If you don't get it set ju-u-u-st right, it will leak and drip and leak and drip.<P>Steve<P>------------------<BR>                     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero1sig.JPG">                   <BR>http://www.kelleyfamilyfieros.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||8
Z||000014||Ken Wittlief||05-21-2001||02:26 PM||||My worst repair job wasnt on a fiero, but I thought I was loosing my mind!<P>My 64VW bug w/sunroof was loosing brake fluid, had to pump the brakes and the resevoir kept getting low.<P>I swept the garage floor real good, got a pint of brake fluid, topped the res off and started pumping the brakes. I looked all under the car to see where it was leaking: Nothing.<P>topped the res off again and pumped it for several minutes: no fluid coming out anywhere.<P> there is a section where the brake line runs inside the car, so I pull the carpet up and take the back seat out: nothing.<P> My now my pint was empty so I took another car and got a quart. I pumped that whole quart of brake fluid through that car and not a drop came out anywhere!<P>  By now Im starting to think there is a pin hole somewhere and the brake fluid is being atomized / coming out as a mist!<P>  Or maybe this is all a bad dream - or maybe God hates me! This was impossible, that brake fluid had to be going somewhere.<P>  I decided to give it up for the day and backed the car out of the garage. When I looked forward there was a trail of brake fluid back to where it had been. So I pushed the car in back to where it had been parked and looked to see where it was coming from.<P>  there is a spot down by the pedals where the breakline goes through the frame, with rubber gromets on both sides. It was leaking in that little 2" section of line inside the frame - I had about 2 quarts in there all together - it had no way to leak out until I backed the car up and it started to slosh around.<P>  Whenever you find yourself in a situation that cant possibly be happening - stop and figure out - if I wanted to CAUSE this to happen to someone as a practical joke how would I do it?<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 05-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Jake_Dragon||05-21-2001||03:45 PM||||Would have to be the rear breaks. I had the front break pads changed the rears still looked fine. Bought a vacuum pump and started to bleed the breaks first caliper went fine next one I broke the bleeder off and nothing was getting it out, so decided to replace the caliper with a rebuilt one. Not wanting to have my breaks out of balance I replaced both rear calipers. I have to admit I do have a lot more stopping power. Now if only the parking break worked.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://home1.gte.net/rebel33/logo.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||rogergarrison||05-21-2001||03:59 PM||||lucky for me front manifold was under recall. The local pontiac dealer replaced it with new, broke off 3 studs. But again lucky for me, that was their problem. Now the unlucky part...while it was there they pinched the spark plug wires off on that side, crushed some other wires and lines back there, and as a favor replaced my 160* thermostat with an OEM 195*. Well it then ran so lousy (3 cyl) and my turbo will only run with a 160*, they burned up the clutch for me. I was furious when I picked it up, manager just told me thats the way it ran when I dropped it off. I told them it ran perfectly when I came back just 8 hours earlier from Dallas Texas, just exhaust leak. so saving on the exhaust only cost me a week with no car, new wires, hoses, repair of the wiring, and a new clutch, fork, t.o. bearing, and flywheel. Needless to say, I fix 95% of everything myself now.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||danny boy||05-21-2001||06:18 PM|||| Going through and fixine everything the previous owner did to my GT. Engine change+ dumb hillbilly= everything was ziptied in place including the main wiring harness to the exaust(this guy was a rocket scientist, let me tell ya), map sensor to manifold, all relays in engine bay, plug wires to looms, and anything else he could find. Instead if the nuts for the bolts that hold the front of the cradle in place he had lugnuts( which I put back on the wheels they were missing from), more zipties n the exaust where the springs should go,and the duct tape holding the intake hoses together and to the engine.<BR>   It took me six hours to piece the wiring harness back together so It would at least run.<P>------------------<BR>don't push the A/c button.. it catches the car on fire     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PFF_sig_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||danny boy||05-21-2001||06:20 PM||||Forgot to mention the fact that he didnt even so much as scuff the factory clearcoat when he painted. Where the paint was flaking off I removed it with a razorblade and it looked better underneath than the new paint did!||||reg||1
Z||000019||AusFiero||05-22-2001||09:46 AM||||When I got my Fiero the plug at the rear near the battery was literally rusted to pieces around the socket connection. Hence could not get it out. Had to drop the engine and cradle to get to it. When we did finally get it out I found out why it had rusted. It was the original plug. Musta just been to hard for mechanics to replace over the years.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> <P>But it did give me a reason to do the clutch and machine the flywheel and replace the main rear engine oil seals while it was all out. ANd a god knows how many other things. Had a dead injector too. How often does that happen hey. Try sourcing one from GM and you will see they don't even expect it to happen. It is a very expensive little part.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero.gif"> <BR>When I was young I spent 80% of my money on Women, Gambling and Alcohol. I guess I wasted the rest.||||reg||1
Z||000020||mbramble||05-22-2001||10:14 AM||||Hey sjp777,<P>Years and years ago (too many to think about!), my uncle had an old 65 Ford Fairlane 500 that he broke a manifold bolt off of and had that blasted ticking sound.  What was his fix, the great mechanic that he was??!!  He clamped a set of vice grip pliers on there and left them there for the next umpteen years that he owned the car!!  No more ticking.  Course he had plenty of room in the engine compartment to add on such accessories.....||||reg||1
Z||000021||Ken Wittlief||05-22-2001||10:22 AM||||Hey look! some bozo left a pair of vice grips clamped in here - I could use another pair<P>SSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSS<P>  aaaaaaaaaaaaaaahhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhggggggggg!<P> muh hands on fire!||||reg||1
Z||000022||Butter||05-22-2001||03:44 PM||||Ken,<BR>I see you learn Kwick.||||reg||12
Z||000023||Neverfinished||05-22-2001||06:01 PM||||Throttle cable and shifter cable were way too much fun. Also the ball joints were a fun time. So much fun working on these cars!<P>------------------<BR>To Be Old And Wise, You First Must Be Young And Stupid.-----------------<BR>It's not a waste of time... If you are wasted at the time||||reg||1
